artur palma and his assistant are taking  no chances  in this funeral parlor that he runs in  lisbon  they're handling the body of an elderly  resident who just died from coronavirus  palmer says he visits nursing homes up  to four times a week to pick up bodies  of those killed by covet 19.  more than 12 thousand people have died  so far in portugal  half of them since the beginning of  january  it's chaos it's really chaos so many  people are dead  there's no room for all of them people  should be careful  they need to know that coronavirus kills  for real  i have already lost my aunt my cousin my  father and my grandfather  portugal is currently the hardest hit  country in the world in proportion to  its population  on top of a nationwide lockdown portugal  has shut its borders with spain  and for two weeks no portuguese citizen  can travel abroad except in special  cases  before the closure there was some  movement in the border area but at the  moment it is quieter  i assume that people are paying  attention to the border controls and  respecting the restrictions  hospitals are severely strained with his  health ministry warning there are only a  few intensive care beds left on the  mainland  some patients have been airlifted to the  island of mandela to ease the pressure  the government's also asked for help  from germany which is sending medical  workers and equipment to help cope with  the influx  the situation is only comparable truly  to  to a tsunami in the sense of the number  of  infections that we are seeing on the  rise um  for weeks onwards and um this of course  then translates into more patients going  in  to er rooms which then leads to more  people being admit admitted into  hospital beds  and then into icu beds and sadly to  higher death rates  the government says the more contagious  strain of coronavirus from the uk  is to blame for up to a third of the  country's infections  but the situation went from bad to worse  as restrictions relaxed over christmas  i think they made a mistake at christmas  they didn't have enough courage to order  people to stay home for political  reasons  people still don't have respect for the  rules and for as long as they don't  understand that they've got to respect  the rules  we're not going anywhere but even if  rules are respected  the government's warned the public that  the country is facing its worst moment  since the start of the pandemic and that  it will continue  for at least a few more weeks emily sue  al jazeera
 

